# DDD Workshop 05

# Observation Domain Discovery

Status:
Discovery Workshop

Purpose:
Discover the true business meaning of an Observation before defining ingestion pipelines, telemetry, integrations, repositories, events, or implementation models.

This workshop intentionally avoids software design.

The objective is to discover the atomic unit of commercial reality.

---

# Workshop Question

What is the smallest objective fact that can exist about an Organization without requiring interpretation?

---

# Historical Thought Experiment

Imagine FLUANZ never existed.

It is 1992.

An experienced commercial auditor spends several weeks inside an organization.

During the investigation the auditor records observations such as:

- Website has no pricing page.
- Average sales response time is 4.7 hours.
- SPF record is missing.
- CRM completeness is 31%.
- No follow-up after enterprise demonstrations.

At this stage the auditor has not reached conclusions.

No recommendations exist.

No findings have been written.

Only objective observations have been collected.

---

# Candidate Definition

An Observation is the smallest objective commercial fact that can be recorded without explanation, interpretation, or judgment.

Observations describe reality.

They do not explain reality.

---

# Constitutional Test

An Observation must satisfy all of the following:

- Objective
- Verifiable
- Atomic
- Organization-scoped
- Timestamped
- Independent of interpretation

If two competent investigators cannot independently arrive at the same recorded fact, it is not an Observation.

---

# Examples

## Valid Observations

- Average response time = 5h 11m
- CRM completeness = 42%
- DMARC policy = None
- Website missing pricing page
- Opportunity has no next scheduled activity
- Win rate declined 8%

These are observable facts.

---

## Invalid Observations

- Sales performance is poor.
- Marketing is ineffective.
- Pipeline quality is weak.
- Customers are losing confidence.

These require interpretation and therefore are not Observations.

---

# First Principles

An Observation:

- Exists independently of FLUANZ.
- Exists independently of an Assessment.
- Exists independently of interpretation.
- Can be verified.
- Can participate in multiple Assessments.
- Can contribute to multiple Evidence packages.

An Observation does not:

- Explain
- Predict
- Recommend
- Judge
- Prioritize

---

# Candidate Characteristics

Every Observation is:

- Atomic
- Objective
- Immutable
- Traceable
- Timestamped
- Organization-scoped

---

# Candidate Sources

Observations may originate from:

- CRM
- Website
- Email Infrastructure
- Marketing Platforms
- Calendar Systems
- Human Interviews
- Uploaded Documents
- Concierge Investigation

The business source matters.

The transport mechanism does not.

---

# Relationship to Organization

Every Observation belongs to exactly one Organization.

Observations cannot cross organizational boundaries.

Organization ownership guarantees commercial accountability.

---

# Relationship to Assessment

Assessments do not create Observations.

Assessments discover, validate, correlate, aggregate, and contextualize Observations.

An Assessment consumes Observations in order to forge Evidence.

---

# Relationship to Evidence

A single Observation is not Evidence.

Evidence emerges only when multiple validated Observations are evaluated together within business context and support or refute a commercial proposition.

Observation

↓

Assessment Investigation

↓

Evidence

---

# Lifecycle

Reality Exists

↓

Observation Occurs

↓

Observation Recorded

↓

Observation Verified

↓

Available for Assessment

↓

Contributes to Evidence

↓

Preserved as Organizational History

Observations remain immutable historical records.

---

# Constitutional Invariants

Every Observation:

- Belongs to one Organization.
- Exists at one point in time.
- Is immutable once recorded.
- Has a discoverable source.
- Can participate in multiple Assessments.
- Can support multiple Evidence packages.
- Cannot directly produce Findings.

---

# Architectural Discovery

The concept of "Signal" was evaluated during this workshop.

Applying the Independent Lifecycle Rule revealed that a Signal possesses:

- No independent identity.
- No unique lifecycle.
- No business invariants.
- No ownership.

A Signal is therefore not promoted to a first-class Domain Entity.

Its responsibilities are absorbed into the Evidence Domain.

Observation

↓

Evidence

↓

Finding

This reduces conceptual complexity while strengthening the domain model.

---

# Open Questions

- Can an Observation ever be corrected?
- Does a correction replace an Observation or create a new Observation?
- Can Observations expire?
- Should confidence exist at the Observation level or only at the Evidence level?
- Can manually collected Observations and automated Observations coexist equally?
- What distinguishes Observation from Domain Event?

---

# Workshop Outcome

This workshop establishes Observation as the atomic unit of commercial truth.

All future reasoning within FLUANZ begins with Observations.

Observations are objective.

Evidence is constructed from Observations.

Findings are derived from Evidence.

Recommendations are generated from Findings.

Executive Decisions are informed by Recommendations.

This progression forms the constitutional reasoning pipeline of FLUANZ.

